4.text:
The C1-C2 black chain is isolated with exactly two liberties (B1/B2), but C2 itself is already at a single liberty (B2). White B2 is therefore legal: it fills C2's last liberty and captures C2 outright while the new white stone retains its own liberty at B1. This leaves the remaining stone at C1 in atari (only B1 remains). Black's sole reply is B1; White then answers at A1 (or B2 if needed after any intervening moves elsewhere) and removes C1 unconditionally.  

B1 as White's first move is legal but fails to force capture: Black safely answers at B2, capturing the single white stone via snapback and restoring the chain's liberties. All other candidate points either leave the target group at least one liberty/escape route (A3 etc.) or fail to reduce it to a single liberty against optimal Black replies. Hence B2 is the unique forcing first move that initiates an unconditional kill sequence.  

